    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3321.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 797,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird and Wonderful)\nWater is famous among scientists for defying many standard rules of chemistry and physics. \n\n* **The Three States of Matter:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical forms: \n  * **Liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (steam, vapor, humidity)\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting vital nutrients and chemicals through living organisms and ecosystems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life and making Earth uninhabitable.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living bodies from overheating.\n* **Surface Tension:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion). This creates surface tension (allowing insects to walk on water) and capillary action (allowing water to travel from the roots of a tall tree up to its highest leaves).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n\n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**.\n* Of that freshwater, the vast majority is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground.\n* Less than **1%** of all Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere for human use.\n\nWater is constantly moving in a continuous loop called the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle): it evaporates from the oceans into the atmosphere, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into the oceans via rivers and streams.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife on Earth likely began in the oceans billions of years ago, and all known life still depends entirely on water. \n\n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. \n* **Biological Functions:** Water regulates human body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste through urine and sweat, and acts as the medium for all cellular chemical reactions. \n* **Agriculture and Industry:** Beyond biology, human civilization relies on water to grow crops, raise livestock, generate electricity (hydroelectric power), manufacture goods, and cook.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the engine of the Earth's climate, the cradle of biological evolution, and the most precious resource necessary for the survival of humanity and all other living things."
